    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d106-a5">

                <pb facs="0254" n="198" sameAs="frus:frus1876/pg198"/>

                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Liverpool</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1875-03-22"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 22,
                        1875</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gentlemen</hi>: 1. Steamships, when under way during
                    a fog, should blow the whistle twice in quick succession every three minutes;
                    the first blast to call attention, and the second to define position.</p>
                <p>The short blasts, to show the position of the helm of meeting or passing
                    steamers, are absolutely necessary, but the one which takes the initiative
                    should have the right of way.</p>
                <p>The remainder of the steam-signals will lead to mischief, as sailors will differ
                    in what makes a long or short note.</p>
                <pb facs="0255" n="199" xml:id="pg199"/>
                <p>2. Sailing-vessels should be compelled to use a bellows fog-horn when under way,
                    as its notes can in quiet weather be heard at a distance of two miles.</p>
                <p>The tin mouth-trumpet is almost useless, and in cold weather blisters the
                    mouth.</p>
                <p>I remain, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>W. W. KIDDLE,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Commander Steamship
                            Celtic</hi>.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Messrs. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ismay, Imrie</hi> &amp; Co.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
